{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/645081-3-new-contracts-critiques-sought-the-hunt-dominion-and-fleeting-dreams","id":645081,"title":"3 new Contracts - Critiques sought : the Hunt, Dominion, and Fleeting Dreams","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-08-30T00:23:55-07:00","id":645081,"title":"3 new Contracts - Critiques sought : the Hunt, Dominion, and Fleeting Dreams","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Greetings all,</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I've drafted 3 antagonist Contracts for my upcoming chronicle. This is my first time trying my hand at writing Contracts, so I'd really appreciate any Critiques or Feedback you can provide.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I'm also uncertain about my dot ratings, cost, and catches, so pointers for those would also be great.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I think I have final versions of these contracts which I really like and work well with the concepts behind them. I'm still a little uncertain of their mechanics as far as balancing goes, so I'd really appreciate critiques of dot ratings, cost, and how well the mechanics work; most especially for Hunt 1 &amp; 2 and Dominion 4 - clauses that have been giving me issues, and which I just reworked.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">[Note: I designed these my home-brewed setting, specifically the Loyalist Courts I'm using. They should still work elsewhere, though. If you're interested or want context, my write-up for that faction can be found </span><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1iN98pJDDaDIw2VmLjaQYAObwHEhUfYq0vsVi7hy0baM/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><u>Here</u></span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I also tried to include both 1e and 2e rules where possible for flexibility.<br /> The spoilers weren't working with the Conditions, so I just removed them rather than having them broken up into 2 balloons.Sorry, I know it disrupts the flow.]</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Thank you for your help, and hope you enjoy</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">~Seraph</span><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><u>Contracts of the Hunt (Loyalists)</u></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Loyalists of any variety are charged with the grim duty of kidnapping humans for their masters in Arcadia; these Contracts were forged by their masters to significantly empower the changeling\u2019s abilities to do so. It is to a dark purpose that changelings learn these Contracts, but merely </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>using </i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">them does not require a Clarity roll - though they do propel the changeling toward choices that do.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Tracing the Fading Footsteps (\u2022) [Troublesome Clause; New Draft - see post #7]</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Use of this Clause ensures that the changeling will be able to find the target again, once they\u2019ve chosen them to be taken by the Others.</span></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Cost: 1 Glamour to mark + 1 Glamour to follow</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dice Pool: Survival + Wyrd versus Wits + Stealth</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Action: Instant and Contested (resistance is reflexive)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Duration: One Season</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Catch: Gift an object to the target; they do not have to accept it, it can merely be placed on their person. This has the Drawback of leading the changeling to the object, however, rather than the target.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The cost to mark is waived for any target with whom the changeling has a Pledge; the cost to follow is waived until the first sunrise after marking a target.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">ROLL RESULTS</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dramatic Failure: The changeling marks another character within eyesight instead of their chosen target. If they gave an object to their intended target, it immediately changes hands through the whim of Fate, unbeknownst to the changeling.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Failure: The changeling is unable to mark the target to be followed, but won\u2019t be aware of this until they attempt to do so.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Success: To activate this Clause, the changeling spends 1 Glamour and rolls for activation to commit one target whom they can see to be followed. From then until the next change of the seasons, the changeling may spend 1 Glamour to be led to the target. They will not know where that location is, they will merely be guided there on the wings of chance. Ordinarily, they will follow the target\u2019s most commonly (or most recently) used route to the their location. The changeling will know when they are approaching line-of-sight with the target, allowing them to stop before they are noticed.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This Clause cannot be used to track targets who are not currently on the same plane of existence.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Exceptional Success: The changeling will get some insight into where the target is at the moment they begin following the target.</span></span></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Spotting the Vulnerable (\u2022\u2022) </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">[Troublesome Clause; New Draft - see post #7]</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Loyalist on the hunt uses this Clause to search out a particular weakness in the crowd, helping them better select the ideal victim for their needs.</span></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Cost: 1 Glamour + 1 Glamour (optional)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dice Pool: Wits + Wyrd versus (highest) Resolve + Subterfuge</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Action: Instant and Contested (resistance is reflexive)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Catch: The changeling poses a riddle or a leading question to the group; it must be at least logically related to the trait they\u2019re looking for. No one is forced to answer, but will have subconscious reactions which reveal who has the sought trait.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">ROLL RESULTS</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dramatic Failure: The changeling focuses in on a detail which they misinterpret for the weakness in question; the target they find not only isn\u2019t vulnerable to the weakness being sought, but likely has a significant advantage over the changeling which they aren\u2019t aware of.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Failure: The changeling finds no one which has the weakness they\u2019re seeking, and isn\u2019t certain if they overlooked something or if no such target it present.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Success: When using this Clause, the changeling specifies a Vice, Flaw, or Derangement (or Condition) which they\u2019re searching for. Upon a success, they are either able to notice in a crowd who has the weakness specified, or they learn that no one in this area does and are refunded the Glamour. Anyone which the trait immediately stands out to the changeling. When they find someone with the desired trait, the changeling has the option to spend an additional Glamour to learn a few details about their particular weakness.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Storytellers are encouraged to allow closely related traits to all be recognized, rather than being overly specific about what needs to be specified: for instance, looking for Gluttony may also find people with an addiction; a player also should not need to specify between mild or severe versions of a derangement.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Exceptional Success: On an exceptional success, if multiple people have the weakness sought, the changeling automatically focus on which one is most exploitable; in addition, they also learn another one of the target\u2019s traits which they can take advantage of.</span></span></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Will-o-the-Wisp (\u2022\u2022\u2022)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The changeling weaves together a simple illusion or a series of them designed to catch a target\u2019s attention and draw them, curious or impassioned, to a location of the changeling\u2019s choosing.</span></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Cost: 1 Glamour per illusion (per roll)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dice Pool: Manipulation + Wyrd versus Intelligence + Composure</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Action: Extended and Contested (each roll represents about 10 seconds)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Catch: </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The changeling has stolen something that the target wants back, and is using that as the illusion.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">ROLL RESULTS</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dramatic Failure: If the changeling dramatically fails a roll or loses a round in the contest by 5 or more successes, the target is clued in that something is wrong about the situation. They may stop following the illusions altogether, or may decide to continue suspecting that it\u2019s a trap.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Failure: On any roll that the changeling fails, the illusion that they made didn\u2019t lure the target any closer, but didn\u2019t lose their interest. Perhaps something started distracting the target, or their illusion appeared in the wrong place or with an off detail which resulted in them not noticing. On the next roll, the changeling takes a -1 penalty.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Success: </span></span> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The changeling weaves together a string of simple, short-lived illusions. They may be the sound of fighting, or crying, or the glitter of gemstones; whatever form the changeling desires. These illusions affect only one or two senses, and last only a couple of rounds - enough to peak the curiosity of their target. Each round in the contest, the changeling creates one such illusion to draw in their target to a desired location. The target number represents how far away and how isolated the changeling is trying to get the target to be: getting them to enter a shop they pass by from drawing their attention to the window may only take 1 success; luring them down a string of alleyways where they have total concealment from casual observers may take 10.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The changeling must have line of sight on both their target and the illusion in order to use this Clause, but may do so indirectly, so as with live video feeds.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Exceptional Success: If the changeling rolls 5 more successes than their target on any roll in the contest, the potent illusion tugs heavily on their heartstrings, causing the target to throw caution to the wind; they take a -2 penalty on further rolls in the contest and to Perception rolls for noticing their surroundings.</span></span></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Divide the Herd</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> (\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022) </span></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">- [thank you AceofAlmonds!]</span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This clause physically separates a target from a group with a barrier. It's not very useful on an open plain, but in a limited space, it can separate a mark from their friends, or provide an effective roadblock against pursuers.</span></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Cost:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> 3 Glamour</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dice Pool:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> Subterfuge + Wyrd - target Dexterity</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Action:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> Instant, contested</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Catch:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> The target is currently lost in a maze (including the Hedge).</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">ROLL RESULTS</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dramatic Failure:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> A barrier springs up, but contrary to the user\u2019s intention (separating them and their target, or trapping them with their pursuers). The barrier is treated as having two success.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Failure:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> No barrier appears.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Success:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> A barrier, up to 50 feet long, 20 feet high, and 1 foot thick, springs up between the target and the nearest group of people, and lasts for the scene. If the barrier intersects with an existing barrier (such as a wall, ceiling, or the Thorns), it stops at that point, but in such a way as there is no gap. The wall looks like it's made out of a logical material for the area: concrete in an alley, rock in a canyon, thick brush in a forest. However, the wall is treated as having a Structure and Durability equal to successes rolled. Climbing rolls take a penalty equal to successes. The barrier acts as full concealment, and muffles sound from the other side.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Exceptional Success:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> No further benefit beyond more successes.</span></span></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Memory\u2019s Forgetful Fog (\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">After successfully capturing someone for the True Fae, their greatest risk is that of discovery - and the retribution that follows. To better cover up their grim taks, they employ this Clause to cloud over suspicious memories in the minds of those closest to the missing victim.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Forming this curse is a complicated weaving of Glamour, looking like the grand curses of the sorceresses in classic fairytales. It takes some time and privacy to complete, and must be done in the target\u2019s home or the last place they were in the mortal realm.</span></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Cost: 3 Glamour + 1 Willpower</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dice Pool: Occult + Wyrd versus Presence + Wryd</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Action: &quot;Instant&quot;; it takes about 15 minutes to complete.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Catch: If a Fetch is left in the place of the target, the Cost is reduced to 2 Glamour. Instead of entirely forgetting the target, those cursed instead forget any unusual circumstances surrounding their disappearance and replacement.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">ROLL RESULTS</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dramatic Failure: The changeling weaves a curse of Glamour, but the effect backfires. Rather than suppressing memories of the target and their disappearance in the minds of those affected, the Glamour highlights the suspicious circumstances and draws increased attention and scrutiny.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Failure: The changeling fails to create the curse. They are aware of the failure, and can attempt it again, however successive attempts take a cumulative -1 penalty.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Success: Upon successful use of this Contract, the changeling creates a curse which fogs memories about the target, pushing them from the minds of those closest to them. Family and coworkers just forget that the target is part of their lives, and avoid thinking about them. This doesn\u2019t suppress any memories, per se but causes those affected to redirect their thoughts and attention. Memories involving the disappearance of the target and unusual circumstances leading up to it are pretty potently blocked. As a consequence, they can notice something is wrong if another person asks about the missing target, which causes them to confront the strange blank spots in their memory.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Once a victim of this curse starts to confront the memory gaps and the fog which causes them to stop thinking about it, they can begin to overcome it. If necessary, this can be handled as an Extended action using Resolve, aiming to achieve the changeling\u2019s Wyrd in successes; however it is best handled as a result of intense therapy or investigation into the matter.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Each success on activating the contract effects one person who was close to the target; the changeling has no knowledge or control over which people are effected, however the curse first targets witnesses to the disappearance or suspicious events leading up to it, and those most likely to notice the disappearance.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Storyteller is encouraged to roll this Clause for the player in secret, to enhance the character\u2019s paranoia over not knowing who, or even how many, they affected.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Exceptional Success: Those affected by this curse will not simply forget the target, but will invent a story to justify their disappearance to themselves; they will share this story to any who ask about the target, thus belaying the suspicions of others.</span></span></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><u>Contracts of Dominion (Unseelie)</u></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The \u201cUnseelie Loyalists\u201d are loyal only to their own power over others - the power of the Fae that the covet. They make use of these Contracts to fill mortals with falsehoods which drive them to becoming beholden to the changeling and the changeling alone.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Whispers of Crippling Doubt (\u2022)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">In order to work their way into the hearts of their target, the changeling spins a Glamour which fills their mind with lies and doubts - of their self and others - making them more pliable to the changeling\u2019s suggestions.</span></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Cost: 1 Glamour</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dice Pool: Manipulation + Wyrd - Target\u2019s Composure</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Action: Instant</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Catch: </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The target has broken their given word to someone within the last week.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">ROLL RESULTS</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dramatic Failure: The changeling overplays their hand; the target instead gains a +2 bonus to resisting their manipulations, and becomes suspicious of their intentions.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Failure: The Glamour fails to take hold, and has no effect on the target.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Success: </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The target suffers a -2 penalty to the manipulations of the changeling for the remainder of the scene. This penalty to applies to their Resistance against Social actions, and may be applicable against some Mental actions as well at Storyteller discretion.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Exceptional Success: </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The target suffers the same penalty to actions they take in opposition to the changeling\u2019s goals (if they have some indication that the changeling would be displeased with the action).</span></span></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">A Poison For Trust (\u2022\u2022)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">To better secure their influence over a mortal, the changeling Glamours them with suspicions against their loved ones, isolating their target from the people that can keep them safe.</span></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Cost: 1 Glamour + 1 Willpower</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dice Pool: Subterfuge + Wyrd versus Composure + Empathy</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Action: Instant and Contested (resistance is reflexive)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Catch: The changeling creates and besmirches a symbol of the relationship (a photo, written name, likeness in a doll, friendship pendant, etc), and casually discards it at the site.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">ROLL RESULTS</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dramatic Failure: The changeling\u2019s lies fall so far off the mark that the target retreats into a deeper relationship with the subject of this Clause. Until the end of the Chapter, the target can restore a lost Willpower point or reroll a failed degeneration roll once by having a meaningful interaction with that person. Of course, if that person betrays them, it will be a powerful blow to their psyche (changelings roll for degeneration at Clarity 2, and mortals acquire the Suspicions derangement or roll for Integrity loss).</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Failure: The Glamour fails to take hold and has no effect on the target. The changeling cannot attempt to poison that relationship again for 24 hours, and future uses of this Clause against the target take a -1 penalty.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Success: The changeling chooses one relationship of their target\u2019s to poison them against with suspicions of manipulation and betrayal.The Glamour casts doubts on all of that person\u2019s motives and actions toward the target, and highlights anything which could be interpreted as manipulative. That person takes a penalty equal to the Changeling\u2019s Wyrd in all social interactions with them for the remainder of the Chapter (or several days).</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The changeling must be aware of the nature of the relationship and who it is with (either seeing them or knowing their name), and must have the target in sight when they activate this Clause.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Exceptional Success: The target also suffers from the Suspicions derangement or condition. Any failed Social roll which the person fails toward the target triggers the effect, in addition to being set off by other accidental misfortunes.</span></span></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Slave of Desire (\u2022\u2022\u2022)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Just as pimps sometimes force drug addiction on their workers to control them, the changeling is able to create a potent emotional addiction in the target with Glamour. What the addiction is to and what the \u201chigh\u201d feels like can be decided, in some degree, by the changeling when they inflict this upon a mortal - and it can be entirely whimsical, though usually it is to an experience related in some way to the fae\u2019s nature.</span></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Cost: 2 Glamour + 1 Willpower</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dice Pool: Presence + Wyrd vs Resolve + Wyrd</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Action: Extended and contested; target is equal to Willpower. Each roll represents one minute.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Catch: The changeling imbibes a highly addictive substance.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">ROLL RESULTS</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dramatic Failure: The changeling himself becomes addicted to the experience he tried to afflict.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Failure: The target resists the attempt to addict them; they become aware of what the changeling was trying to accomplish, and may bolster their will against any further attempts: the target adds their Composure to future rolls to resist this Clause.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Success: In order to use this contract, the changeling must provide the experience they hope to addict the target to, while coaxing them with Glamour and force of personality into total intoxication. When the changeling reaches a number of successes equal to the target\u2019s Willpower, the victim is cursed with addiction. The experience could be anything from listening to a particular song to holding burning charcoal. Generally, the changeling will try to choose something that only they can provide it, however the experience must be general enough that anybody else </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>could</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> provide it, if they know what it is.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Exceptional Success: In addition to creating the addiction in the target\u2019s mind, the target also associates the changeling with the high feelings from the experience, granting the changeling +1 to Social rolls against the target for as long as they remain addicted.</span></span></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Condition: Glamoured Addiction (Persistent)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Your character is addicted to experience chosen and created by a fae. While not as potent as a chemical addiction, the experience does provide an enchanting sense of bliss and relief. You need to indulge your addiction regularly to keep it under control; being unable to feed your addiction for a period equal to [11 minus the Wyrd of the fae who caused it] days can result in the Deprived Condition.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Resolution:</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> Regain a dot of Integrity, lose another dot of Integrity, or achieve an exceptional success on a breaking point. Finding a way to relieve the symptoms of deprivation while not giving into the addiction for an extended period of time will end its hold on you.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Beat:</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> Your character chooses to get a fix rather than fulfill an obligation.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Condition: Deprived</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Your character suffers from an addiction. She is unable to get her fix, however, leaving her irritable, anxious, and unable to focus. Remove one from her relevant dice pools: at minimum this affects Resolve, and Composure based actions, but other Mental or Social actions may also be penalized at Storyteller discretion. This does not influence derived traits.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Resolution:</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> Your character indulges her addiction.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Beat:</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> n/a</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Derangement: Glamoured Addiction</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Your character is hooked on a experience chosen and created by a fae. If he doesn\u2019t get it regularly, he gets anxious and goes out of his way to satisfy his need, even neglecting more important duties. If the opportunity to indulge arises, the character must roll Resolve + Composure to resist. After a period of [11 - the Wyrd of the fae which caused this] days, the character suffers withdrawal symptoms of anxiety, irritability, and lack of focus. This applies a -1 penalty to most Mental or Social actions, especially Resolve or Composure based dice pools.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">As with mundane addictions, this can be beaten by prolonged withdrawal from the experience, and usually requires finding someway to relieve the symptoms of deprivation.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Gift of Entrapment (\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022) </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">[Troublesome Clause; New Draft - see post #7]</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The changeling spins together something their target desires out of scraps and Glamour, earning their trust with a gift, while driving them further into reliance on the changeling\u2019s help.</span></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Cost: 2 Glamour + 1 Willpower</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dice Pool: Crafts + Wyrd (versus Resolve + Empathy; optional)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Action: Instant; contested at the time of the next misfortune (optional)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Catch: The target expresses a wish or desire out loud before they notice the changeling.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">ROLL RESULTS</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dramatic Failure: The changeling apparently produces something which the target needs to accomplish something they desire. The Glamour holds for just long enough for them to attempt to use it, at which point it is revealed as scraps - which itself may get them into further trouble. The target knows the changeling peddled fool\u2019s gold.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Failure: The changeling can create nothing useful. Repeated attempts suffer a -1 penalty (cumulative). The target may also stop believing that they can assist, and ignore them.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Success: The changeling produces something which the target desires, preferably to get them out of trouble of some kind. The changeling must have at least general knowledge of what would help to attempt this. If their mark accepts the gift, it becomes as good as the real thing, and does indeed accomplish whatever they were hoping for. However, it guarantees that another misfortune soon befalls them, and that they will think of the changeling in trying to solve it; this does not necessarily force them to return to the changeling, but will give them the impression that they can help in this circumstance too\u2026</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">A Resolve + Empathy roll may be required to resist returning, at Storyteller discretion, which must meet or beat the successes earned on the activation of this Clause.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">If the target does return, the changeling suffers a cumulative -2 penalty if they attempt to use this Clause again to solve the next problem. It works to best effect if they have other means of helping\u2026 once the debt has been accumulated, of course.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Exceptional Success: For the remainder of the Chapter (or several days), the changeling gains a +2 bonus [or 1 Door is automatically opened] in attempts to convince the target to do something.</span></span></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Courting Forgiveness (\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">If ever the changeling is revealed to have harmed, manipulated, or otherwise betrayed someone they seek to dominate, swift use of this Clause bribes them back into the target\u2019s trust and good graces, glossing over any malign qualities of the changeling.</span></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Cost: 3 Glamour + 1 Willpower</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dice Pool: Subterfuge + Wyrd versus Resolve + Composure</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Action: Instant and Contested (resistance is reflexive)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Catch: The changeling\u2019s offer satisfies the target\u2019s Virtue or one of their Aspirations.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">ROLL RESULTS</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dramatic Failure: The target is irrevocably scorned. They become hostile toward the changeling, and are unlikely to ever be convinced to change their opinion. They will seek to undermine the changeling however they can.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Failure: The Glamour has no effect, and the changeling\u2019s deceptive offer likely angers the target.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Success: </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The changeling provides an explanation, a gift, or a promise to the target, laced thickly with a Glamour that causes them to forgive and gloss over any past behavior or quality of the changeling\u2019s which they objected to. The changeling must offer one of these to the target, and must be aware of their current hostility and what provoked it, in order to use this Contract. The target must also have once held an Excellent impression of the changeling. If successful, all past grievances are forgiven in the target\u2019s mind, not merely the one which set them off; the target\u2019s impression of them immediately returns to Excellent.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This impression will not fade, however the target is under no compulsion to keep such a good impression of the changeling; their permanent \u201cnatural\u201d impression is merely returned to Excellent. They can still be swayed against the changeling, with difficulty, and the changeling may ruin this impression through their own actions. This Clause may only be successfully used against a target twice; their third betrayal is the last straw...</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Exceptional Success: The target has a Perfect impression of the changeling for the remainder of the Scene. Any future attempts to convince them of the changeling\u2019s ill intentions suffers a -3 penalty, at least until the changeling directly harms the target.</span></span></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><u>Contracts of Fleeting Dreams</u></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> (Seelie)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Those in the Seelie Court of Loyalists, see their masters as being archetypes of ideals which would improve the mortal world, if only they could bring their Stories here. This Contract was forged to twist mortal hope, belief, and dreams, the better to spin fairy tales out of their lives.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Each Loyalist holds a particular story and ideal above every other as their source of inspiration and hope. This conviction makes it more difficult for them to use these Contracts to spin different stories. As such, any use of these Contracts which follow the interest of their House take a -1 penalty; the Seelie Court in general, a -2 penalty; and all other uses, a -3 penalty - only using these Contracts to be in line with their chosen story are unpenalized. Uses which weave a story in contradiction to the one they exalt may be grounds for a breaking point against Clarity 4, at Storyteller discretion.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Heart Reveals (\u2022)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The changeling learns the details of one of the target\u2019s wishes for their life. The changeling selects how deep a dream they learn before activating this Clause (see modifiers below).</span></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Cost: 1 Glamour</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dice Pool: Wits + Wyrd vs. Composure + Wyrd</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Action: Instant; Contested (resistance is reflexive)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Catch: </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The target has confided in the changeling within the last week.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">ROLL RESULTS</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dramatic Failure: The changeling receives false information, either learning a hope that the target does not have, or getting the details of it completely wrong.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Failure: The changeling learns nothing about the target.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Success: The changeling learns one of the target\u2019s Aspirations or another kind of goal; usually, anyone else involved and what is currently preventing the goal are also revealed. See the modifiers below for the types of goals which can be sought out.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Exceptional Success: The changeling learns an additional goal at that level or one above it.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">[Modifiers: -- immediate concern; -1 short term goal; -2 dream for the future; -3 wishful thinking] </span></span></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Mask of Glamour</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">(\u2022\u2022)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Using this contract, the changeling wraps dreamstuff around a target in a way very reminiscent of a Mask. The effect isn\u2019t strong enough to alter appearance; instead it has the very subtle impact of provoking a particular emotion in people they interact with. The changeling chooses what emotion manifests when they weave this Contract; everyone who interacts with the target feels the same emotion, and that emotion can\u2019t be changed after the Contract is used. Although, due to its short-lived effect, a persistent changeling can follow the target to compel different emotions in different people, wreaking havoc with their social life. The emotional response people have is not overwhelming - they aren\u2019t compelled to act on it - but it does change their perceptions and attitudes toward a person, and may inspire action, depending on the inclinations of those affected.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The changeling must have line-of-sight to use this Contract on target; any other magical effects which affect people's\u2019 emotion toward the target provokes a Clash of Wills.</span></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Cost: 2 Glamour; + 1 Willpower (optional)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dice Pool: Manipulation + Wyrd vs Wits + Wyrd</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Action: instant, contested (resistance is reflexive)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Catch: Used upon the target\u2019s first meeting with a significant person (first meetings after a long absence </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>do </i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">also apply).</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">ROLL RESULTS</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dramatic Failure: Through the Changeling\u2019s mismanagement of glamour, or perhaps from the target investing their own hopes and fears into the Mask, the magic backfires, and takes the opposite effect.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Failure: No effect. Further attempts to use this contract on the target this scene take a -1 penalty.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Success: </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The glamour wraps around the target, and invests a very subtle Mask (or a subtle change to the Mask, in the case of changeling targets) which causes anyone they interact with to feel the designated emotion towards them. This manifests as a +/- 2 modifier on the target\u2019s social interactions. Those affected by interacting with the target have their emotions and perceptions of them distorted by the emotion, but are not compelled to take any action. In cases where it is particularly tempting to act on the emotion, those affected can roll Composure + a relevant Skill to resist the urge.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This clause lasts for one meaningful social interaction that the target has, which could range between an instant action or a few minutes. This might mean a brief chat with a stranger, or several minutes of conversation; in general, only social interactions the target has reason to care about apply. Prolonged interactions, such a job interview or a speech, may count as one scene not one social interaction, but the opening remarks could be tainted by the emotion - which may leave an impression that lasts. A Willpower point can be spent to increase this duration to the length of a Scene.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Exceptional Success: The curse lasts for 1 scene, or 1 chapter if a point of Willpower is spent.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">[Modifiers: the changeling has affinity with the emotion (Virtue/Vice, Mantle) +1] </span></span></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Answers in Dreams (\u2022\u2022\u2022)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">By manipulating the dreams of the target, the changeling can create associations between feelings, situations, individuals, and belief. The associations made in dreams affect the target\u2019s interpretation of the world, and can be used to change their feelings, reactions, or beliefs. This forced association will unravel with each night of restful dreams; however, if the changeling repeatedly changes the target\u2019s dreams, the association can become permanently ingrained into their subconscious.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Loyalists also make regular use of this Clause to shape mortal dreams in a way that allows the True Fae to enter, in mimicry of their Dream Poisoning techniques. This usually involves investing a meaningful association with the Fae\u2019s Title, but can also be done by investing the wonder of Arcadia.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Changelings, and others who have complete control of their dreams (Lucid Dreamer merit, or other supernatural powers) are not automatically immune to these attempts to change their dreams, but they are far more capable of fighting them off. The intruder has to succeed a Wits + Subterfuge roll versus the victim\u2019s Wits + Empathy + Wyrd to even attempt the change, and the victim may decide to alter the dream anyway. It takes a subtle and persistent fae to tamper with the dreams of a changeling.</span></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Cost: 2 Glamour</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dice Pool: Intelligence + Empathy + Mantle vs Resolve + Composure</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Action: extended, contested (resistance is reflexive): goal of target\u2019s Willpower</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Catch: Successful use of \u201cForging the Dream\u201d (Dream \u2022\u2022) removes the need for this cost.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">ROLL RESULTS</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dramatic Failure: The changeling creates an emotional association, but far from the one they were intending to make. The target gains the \u201cDelusional\u201d condition or derangement, believing that the changeling is trying to harm them, or another belief which hampers the changeling\u2019s goals. The changeling cannot attempt to change this delusion through further use of this Contract, and the delusion will not fade over time as usual - they have to prove to the target that they are not out to get to them through mundane means.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Failure: The changeling fails to create an emotional association through the target\u2019s dreams. They may not attempt to influence the same subject in their target again until the next time they sleep - although other subjects can be influenced.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Success: </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The target gains the \u201cDelusional\u201d condition or derangement (see below). The delusion associates one subject with one emotion, each of the changeling\u2019s choosing. The delusion will quickly fade away as the target has natural sleep: after one successful use, the delusion will fade after the next full night of unaltered dreams.</span></span> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The changeling may choose to reinforce the same delusion over several nights with repeated use of this Clause. Each additional successful use (on the same target, regarding the same subject) increases the duration of the delusion by one night of natural sleep. If the changeling reinforces the same delusion for a number of sequential nights exceeding the target\u2019s permanent Willpower, the delusion becomes so ingrained as to become a permanent part of the target\u2019s personality. If the target is allowed any nights of natural rest before reaching this target, the delusion can be prolonged, but won\u2019t become permanent. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Multiple delusions can be forced on one target, but add a cumulative -1 penalty to the activation roll; delusions which affect the same subject in a single target\u2019s mind provoke a Clash of Wills. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Exceptional Success: </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The delusion will last until an additional night of natural sleep; this counts as another sequential use of the Contract, for the purposes of establishing a permanent emotional association.</span></span></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Condition: Delusional (persistent)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The character has made an emotional association that isn\u2019t actually true. They don\u2019t actually hallucinate anything which would confirm this belief, but may misinterpret situations to find evidence which isn\u2019t there. Characters are not forced to act on their belief, but it will affect how they perceive and act. The character may spend a point of Willpower to insist that a sign confirming their association doesn\u2019t actually apply (although it may sound crazy or willfully oblivious to others).</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Resolution:</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> The character completely disproves their delusion.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Beat:</i> </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Each time the character has the opportunity to shake off the influence, but chooses to reinforce it instead. By doing so, the next night of sleep does not count as having natural, unaltered dreams.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Derangement: Delusional (mild)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> The character believes something that isn't actually true; primarily an emotional association with a subject. They won\u2019t hallucinate anything which would confirm this belief, but may misinterpret situations to find evidence which isn\u2019t there. If the character is presented with an appropriate opportunity to act on this belief, they must roll Resolve + Composure to avoid doing so; this roll may be modified with how likely the delusion may be in the situation, or how tempting the situation makes it. The character will always interpret a relevant situation as being in-line with their delusion, unless they spend a point of Willpower to think clear-headed. This derangement can only be cured by being completely disproven.</div></div> </div> </div></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Derangement: Delusional Psychosis (severe) - [included for fun; can't be inflicted or upgraded to]</span></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">As above, however all rolls to resist acting on the delusion <i>only</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> use Composure; the character cannot spend Willpower to resist thinking or interpreting the situation through the context of their beliefs; and any attempt to convince them they\u2019re wrong are reflexively contested by the character\u2019s Resolve + Composure (the character may spend Willpower on this roll to add </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>or subtract</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> dice).</span></span></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Freezing the River of Dreams (\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022) [Clause found - thank you AceofAlmonds!]</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This clause can protect a target from having their hopes manipulated, either to protect them from rival Seelie courtiers or prevent any hard work from being undone.</span></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Cost:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> 2 Glamour</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dice Pool:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> Presence + Expression + Mantle (-target Resolve if resisted)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Action:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> Instant</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Catch:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> The target has asked the user to visit their dreams, willingly and uncoerced</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">ROLL RESULTS</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dramatic Failure:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> The target is warded, but only against the changeling that used the Contract.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Failure:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> The target remains unwarded.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Success:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> The target is warded against attempts to manipulate their goals and hopes. Mundane attempts fail; supernatural attempts suffer a die penalty equal to successes rolled; this does not apply to the changeling who used this Clause on the target. This protection lasts for a number of days equal to the changeling\u2019s rating in Mantle (Seelie Court). </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">If one of the character\u2019s goals is achieved in that time, they may only move on to another goal if it has been part of their on-going goals prior to the use of this Contract; characters may not change goals based on information learned since the activation of this Clause.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Exceptional Success:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> The ward lasts for one lunar month.</span></span></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Woven into the Wyrd (\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022) - [Option A - for games with Talecrafting]</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The changeling is always able to use Cruel Twists of Fate which target mortals; doing so always counts as Forcing the Narrative, even if they seem to be following a narrative theme. Furthermore, the changeling is able to force narrative roles upon humans, for a time. This Clause can only be used once per Chapter, but does </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>not</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> count as the Changeling\u2019s use of Tale-Crafting.</span></span><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Cost: 3 Glamour; or 5 Glamour + 1 Willpower</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dice Pool: Wyrd vs Resolve</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Action:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Instant, contested (resistance is reflexive)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Catch:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The changeling \u2018invests\u2019 the use of this Contract into an object; the first person to accept the object - literally or symbolically - becomes the target (they do not need to be aware of the consequences). Doing so reduces the cost to 1 Glamour + 1 Willpower, and applies a -2 penalty to the target\u2019s resistance.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">ROLL RESULTS</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dramatic Failure: The changeling\u2019s rash use of the Wyrd causes it to recoil and spurn him. The changeling suffers a Cruel Twist of Fate.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Failure: The Contract fails, and the changeling may not make the same attempt again.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Success: the Changeling Succeeds in binding the mortal in the Wyrd. As per a reversal of fate (Swords at Dawn, p. 64), they suffer a Cruel Twist of Fate.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Alternatively, the changeling may apply a Fatebound merit to the target (Swords at Dawn, p. 89) which persists for one chapter (or one game session), in which the target should receive both the benefits and drawbacks of the merit. In order to apply any Fatebound merit, the changeling must purchase it with Experience, although doing so does not apply its effects to the changeling.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Any successful use of this clause is a Breaking Point at Clarity 4, and risks the changeling become addicted to Tale-Crafting (Swords at Dawn, p. 75).</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Exceptional Success: If a Reversal of Fate, the target loses one point of Willpower. If used to apply a Fatebound merit, the mortal continues to be affected by the merit for the rest of the Story - although this does not guarantee repeated or more dramatic use of the associated benefits and drawbacks.</span></span></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Consumed by the Inner Story <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">(\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022) -[Option B - thank you AceofAlmonds]</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The changeling can cause a latent story to consume another, forcing the target to follow the path they were meant for.</span></span><div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Cost: </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">3 Glamour + 1 Willpower</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dice Pool:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> Presence + Subterfuge + Mantle vs Resolve + Wyrd</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Action:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> Instant and contested</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Catch:</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">ROLL RESULTS</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dramatic Failure:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> The target realizes the user is trying to manipulate them, and what role was desired. They feel no compulsion to follow this role, and gain strong mistrust of the user.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Failure:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> The target remains uncompelled.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Success:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> The target is compelled to act out a narrative role the changeling chooses, such as Coward, Sidekick, Villain, or Hero. This cannot cause the target to commit sins below half of their Clarity or equivalent trait, nor can it cause the target to act in a way that would trigger a personal Breaking Point. See the list of suggested modifiers below for guidelines on the effect\u2019s duration. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The target may spend a point of Willpower to resist acting upon this compulsion; the amount of time is proportional to the duration: 1 action if it lasts 5 minutes; 1 minute for a scene or several hours; 1 scene for a day or longer.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Any successful use of this Clause is considered a Breaking Point at Clarity 4. At Storyteller discretion, it may also count as a Breaking Point for the target.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Exceptional Success:</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> The target also gains an additional 1 to 3 points of Willpower, based on the duration (as above), to be spend on actions which are iconic of the narrative role forced upon them; these points may exceed the character\u2019s maximum Willpower pool; any unused points will vanish when the Contract\u2019s effect expires.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Suggested Modifiers:</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> The target sees themself as this role already +2; the idea for this role has been planted by the user previously +1; the role is only a small part of the target's personality -1; the role is completely opposite the target's normal behavior -3 | </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Duration Modifiers:</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> 5 minutes +1; one scene -; four hours -1; one day -2; 2 days -3</span></span></div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Second Chance for<br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=709\" data-tempid=\"temp_709_1572548741915_354\" /><br /> A Beautiful Madness</div>","edited":{"userid":425,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","user":"Seraph Kitty","date":". 09-27-2015, 07:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","name":"Seraph Kitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar425_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-02T21:27:47-07:00","id":649737,"title":"","content":"These are all neat; I really like the concept of loyalist courts, and the powers are things that the game doesn't have, but fit in well. If I don't say anything in the notes below, assume I think it's great <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Notes on Contracts of the Hunt:<br /> 1: How long does the tracking-sense last after activation? Comparing to Four Directions 1 (which lets you choose anyone, but only lasts an hour), I'd have it last until the next sunrise/sunset. Of course, the power as a whole feels like a more complicated version of Four Directions 1; maybe you'd want to just lift that power instead. For the catch, I'd pick one or the other of what's listed (either track a gifted object for free, or track targets pledged with the user for free).<br /> 2: I'd change the Catch to: <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The changeling poses a riddle or a leading question to the group related to the trait they're looking for, and at least one member of the group responds (even if the response doesn't answer the question)</span></span>. I would also not refund the Glamour. Otherwise, this power is fun <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> 3: I would change this to still be contested, but not extended, and have the illusion last a number of rounds equal to successes (or until the target reaches the illusion and discovers it's not real; i.e. failing to pick up fake gold coins). If you keep it as a contest, I would say exactly how long each roll is (3 rounds? That's about 10 seconds). Either way, I'd restrict both the illusion and target to line-of-sight, but allow that line-of-sight to be remote, like security cameras. Catch: The user has stolen something the target wants back, and uses the stolen object as the illusion.<br /> 4: I agree that this clause is weak; it's effects are something that's not hard to produce completely mundanely (i.e. surprising a target with a cosh).<br /> 5: I would have this be Contested rather than Resisted, as it's very powerful (maybe Resolve + Wyrd). It might be interesting if the clause only affects those close to the target, but not more casual acquaintances. <br /> <br /> Hunt 4 proposal: Divide the Herd<br /> This clause physically separates a target from a group with a barrier. It's not very useful on an open plain, but in a limited space, it can separate a mark from their friends, or provide an effective roadblock against pursuers.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> Cost: 3 Glamour<br /> Dice Pool: Subterfuge + Wyrd - target Dexterity<br /> Action: Instant, contested<br /> Catch: The target is currently lost in a maze (including the Hedge).<br /> <br /> Dramatic Failure: A barrier springs up, but between the user and their target, with the same stats as a success.<br /> Failure: No barrier appears.<br /> Success: A barrier, up to 50 feet long, 20 feet high, and 1 foot thick, springs up between the target and the nearest group of people, and lasts for the scene. If the barrier intersects with an existing barrier (such as a wall, ceiling, or the Thorns), it stops at that point, but in such a way as there is no gap. The wall looks like it's made out of a logical material for the area: concrete in an alley, rock in a canyon, thick brush in a forest. However, the wall is treated as having a Structure and Durability equal to successes rolled. Climbing rolls take a penalty equal to successes. The barrier acts as full concealment, and muffles sound from the other side.<br /> Exceptional Success: No further benefit beyond more successes.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Notes on Contracts of Dominion:<br /> 1: This feels too powerful as-written; I would limit the penalty to resisting social manipulation only. Also, I would not have it powered by Manipulation; maybe Subterfuge (or Persuasion) instead. Catch: The target has broken their given word to someone in the past week. (because you can't fool an honest man)<br /> 2: I don't have any issues with this one.<br /> 3: Or this one.<br /> 4: What is the something produced from? Similar materials, whatever the user has on hand, pure thought? There should be some kind of limit on what can be produced (a la Dreams 4 or Fleeting Spring 4), either by value or by complexity (or both).<br /> 5: Can this only be used on targets that once had an Excellent or better impression of the user (since the impression &quot;returns&quot; to Excellent)? Does the effect have a time limit? Can this effect be broken? (I would say yes; a week; with great effort, respectively)<br /> <br /> Notes on Contracts of Fleeting Dreams: (I'd be worried about confusing this with Contracts of Dreams, and would suggest renaming it. Maybe Contracts of Hope? Contracts of Aspiration? Contracts of Bidden Fate?)<br /> 1: Catch: The target has confided in the Changeling within the past week. OR Catch: The Changeling has observed, but not changed, the target's dreams within the past week. (I like that the rolls match with Fleeting Spring 1, rather than Fleeting Autumn 1 or Fleeting Winter 1.)<br /> 2: &quot;<span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Victims may roll have to roll Composure (+ Skill)&quot; - should that be &quot;Victims may roll Composure (+Skill)&quot; or &quot;Victims may have to roll Composure (+Skill)&quot;? Also, victims means those who are interacting with the target, not the target themselves, correct? </span></span><br /> 4: I personally don't like this power, as I think it's too similar to Dream 2, and not powerful enough for a 4-dot. 3-dot would be better. The &quot;stages&quot; thing is a little unclear: once the stages exceed Willpower dots, does the condition become permanent and not decrease ever? Or does it continue decreasing? And short of many exceptional successes, how would this be possible? I'd change the catch to be something that isn't &quot;use this other Contract&quot;. Maybe: The target was suffering from exhaustion before falling asleep. (i.e. weird dreams come when you're super-tired)<br /> 5: From the perspective of using this power in other games, I don't like it, because Talecrafting is an optional rule (unlike dreams or something). I'm not very familiar with those mechanics, but compared to other 5-dots, it seems weak. Some kind of 'consumed and bidden by hope/forced to fulfill the story' might work instead (similar to Fleeting Spring, Summer, and Winter 5, being consumed by the emotion of the court, see below).<br /> <br /> Fleeting Dreams 4 proposal: Freezing the River of Dreams<br /> This clause can protect a target from having their hopes manipulated, either to protect them from rival Seelie courtiers or prevent any hard work from being undone.<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> Cost: 2 Glamour<br /> Dice Pool: Presence + Expression + Mantle (-target Resolve if resisted)<br /> Action: Instant<br /> Catch: The target has asked the user to visit their dreams, willingly and uncoerced<br /> <br /> Dramatic Failure: The target is warded, but only against the changeling that used the Contract.<br /> Failure: The target remains unwarded.<br /> Success: The target is warded against attempts to manipulated their goals and hopes. Mundane attempts fail; supernatural attempts suffer a die penalty equal to successes rolled. This lasts until the next sunset, and does not apply to the person who used the Contract.<br /> Exceptional Success: The ward lasts a week.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Fleeting Dreams 5 proposal: Consuming Flames of Truth<br /> The changeling can cause a latent story to consume another, forcing the target to follow the path they were meant for.<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> Cost: 3 Glamour + 1 Willpower<br /> Dice Pool: Presence + Subterfuge + Mantle vs Resolve + Wyrd<br /> Action: Instant and contested<br /> Catch:<br /> <br /> Dramatic Failure: The target realizes the user is trying to manipulate them, and what role was desired. They feel no compulsion to follow this role, and gain strong mistrust of the user.<br /> Failure: The target remains uncompelled.<br /> Success: For rounds equal to successes + user's Wyrd, the target is compelled to act out a role, such as Coward, Sidekick, Villain, or Hero. This cannot cause the target to commit sins below half their Morality/Clarity/equivalent score. This cannot cause the target to act in a way that would trigger a personal Breaking Point. <br /> Exceptional Success: No benefit beyond longer effects.<br /> Modifiers: The target sees themself as this role already +2; the idea for this role has been planted by the user previously +1; the role is only a small part of the target's personality -1; the role is completely opposite the target's normal behavior -3<br /> </div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5695-aceofalmonds","name":"AceOfAlmonds","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5695_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"323"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-07T14:00:29-07:00","id":655494,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AceOfAlmonds</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/645081-3-new-contracts-critiques-sought-the-hunt-dominion-and-fleeting-dreams?p=649737#post649737\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">These are all neat; I really like the concept of loyalist courts, and the powers are things that the game doesn't have, but fit in well. If I don't say anything in the notes below, assume I think it's great <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Thank you so very much for that critique; it was immensely helpful, and you have some excellent ideas to improve them. In particular, all of the Catches you proposed are excellent. If you don\u2019t mind continuing the conversation, I\u2019d like to discuss some of my thought behind the Contracts to further refine them.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I know that my biggest fault with most of these was making them too complicated; I was trying to balance a few things, and a lot was lost - I\u2019d really like to simplify a few of things, but I\u2019m not sure the best way to do that, while still getting the effects I had in mind...</span><br /> <br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Hunt 1</span></b><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It is pretty much a more complicated (and costly) version of Four Directions 1, I will admit. My aim was making it a \u201cstalk the prey\u201d power, where you designate who is going to be followed, and then can start tracking them down at any point. What I kind of had in mind is that they spend an entire scene following in the footsteps of the target, and it ending when they catch up to the target - about an hour later sounds right. So, for the extra cost, the Clause gives additional insight into their target through the places they might regularly visit.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I had thought the cost was still a bit high - hence the exceptions worked into the Catch, but it is unnecessarily complicated.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b>Hunt 2</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This was one I had trouble with, mostly because it started becoming both too broad and too narrow as I was writing it. I think it\u2019s a pretty solid power, but I feel the need to simplify the language, and possibly broaden what can be scanned for. Maybe something like \u201cchoose the circumstances you want to exploit, and find who that applies to in a crowd\u201d - like physical impairment, gullible, has a compulsion, will chase after something \u2026. The problem is that gets into really fuzzy territory; better suited for a Fate system. But I think using broader categories will make the cost more bearable, and then increasing the optional cost to learn the details of the weakness. Thoughts?</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I want to avoid the situation of characters fruitlessly playing 20 questions while blowing through Glamour - which, given the number of Vices, Flaws, and Derangements, is fairly likely. Depending on the crowd they\u2019re staking out, they could search for OCD, impulsively greedy, suffers addiction\u2026 and not find anyone that applies. That\u2019s why I included the refund if they were successful, but found no one - justifying in-character as nibbling occasional Glamour while you lie in wait in a crowd.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b>Hunt 4</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Your suggestion is an excellent replacement. My thought was going for an attack power which provided an overwhelming advantage when properly set up, and a moderate advantage in other situations\u2026 it didn\u2019t work well, and came out far too weak. Your idea, though, is definitely on theme, awesome, and useful - I\u2019m almost certainly going for that.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b>Hunt 5</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">That was more-or-less the idea. I wouldn\u2019t expect it to get many successes on average, so only a couple people would have their memory\u2019s blanked. Acquaintances are likely going to be unaffected. But I did want it to hit strangers which may have witnessed the crime, for instance, so I described it as affecting \u201cthose most likely to notice the target missing\u201d first (ie, close relationships, coworkers, witnesses), while leaving acquaintances to mess up the curse.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b>Dominion 4</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I was thinking of it being fabricated from scraps and junk and the glamour of the target\u2019s desire (intentionally mimicking Fetch creation). I wanted it to be pretty variable - allowing them to produce money, a weapon, a piece of gossip, maybe a password - so that it could apply to a range of situations of \u201csolves an immediate goal; a bigger problem comes up\u201d.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Do you think a limit of 1 success per dot of resources or equivalent merit the object would require - with modifiers based on how exclusive it is, would work? For example, a password could be produced, but the access it grants might be equivalent to 4 dots of status, and the password might only be known by one division chief. So the contract would require 4 successes as at a -3 penalty. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b>Dominion 5</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Yes, it\u2019s intended to only be used on targets which once had an excellent impression of the user. I wasn\u2019t planning on there being a time limit though, as I don\u2019t think it would make a difference one way or the other, from the way I intended it to work: the changeling does something nice for the target, and all past transgressions are forgiven - not necessarily forgotten. There\u2019s not really going to be a point where the target switches back to being angry, or if there is (as you suggested, a week) it\u2019d be long enough that the changeling will totally prevent that by reinforcing the good impression and control over the target. On the other hand, it\u2019s not a magically-binding impression, so if the changeling messes up again, or through great effort of loved ones, the target can once again be clued into the user\u2019s maliciousness.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It\u2019s meant to be a \u201cI\u2019m sorry I said something wrong; here\u2019s some flowers\u201d contracts - everything\u2019s back to fine, for now, but it remains part of a pattern that can add fuel to the fire the next time they get upset. Make sense, or do think that should still be reworked?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b>Fleeting Dreams 2</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Yes, victims are the ones interacting with the target, who are affected by the emotional impression. The wording should probably be made more clear - the Contract won\u2019t force anyone to take action, just to have an emotional reaction. In cases where it\u2019s really tempting to act on it, they can roll Composure + a relevant skill to resist.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b>Fleeting Dreams 3 (changed from 4)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This is another one that got too complicated and could better explained. It\u2019s supposed to work as if they use the contract every night, the \u201cstages\u201d won\u2019t decrease, and it\u2019ll eventually become permanent. It\u2019s only if they don\u2019t warp their dreams for a night that it starts fading.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Here\u2019s how I imagined it working: the changeling goes into someone\u2019s dreams and fidgets with the symbolic associations to make them afraid of a co-worker, as if they had subconsciously picked up on cues that something was \u201coff\u201d about them. They hadn\u2019t, so if they have a natural / unaltered night of dreaming again, things will revert back to normal. Or the changeling can keep going in and reinforcing that feeling of unease (perhaps with the target playing into the fear, and reinforcing it through their own actions), until it just becomes a permanent association in the target\u2019s mind that they should be afraid of that person, even without a good reason.<br /> Does that make sense, and do you think it works?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b>Fleeting Dreams 4 (your suggestion)</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This is an excellent suggestion, and I greatly appreciate it.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I feel like it should include a hefty drawback, however - such as making the target so rigid in holding that belief that they can\u2019t learn or change at all, even from the natural ways they\u2019re supposed to. Or perhaps making it a \u201ccapstone\u201d by increasing its duration, but also penalizing the changeling who used it from future alterations. Maybe longer-lasting but only protects one goal/hope. I don\u2019t know; I feel like it should be tweaked a little, but I\u2019m not sure why or how - it\u2019s pretty solid as is. Any thoughts?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b>Fleeting Dreams 5</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I can understand your reluctance to tie it into Tale-crafting. That\u2019s not going to be a concern in my chronicle, but I\u2019ll definitely include your alternate version as an option for other games. The duration for it is pretty short, though. Lasting only a couple of seconds, it could throw a huge wrinkle into a scene, but won\u2019t really impact the story or the target\u2019s development. I might model it off of Fleeting Spring 5 instead - having it start at 5 minutes, and last up to 2 days, at mounting penalties.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Being consumed by the emotion of the court was kind of what I was going for with applying a Fatebound Title to the target: these are merits with a significant boon and drawback based on an archetypal story role. </span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Also, being able to use Reversal of Fate against mortals is going to be a huge thing for the Seelie Court in my chronicle; it allows you to create major complications for people who boast or accidentally fall in line with a narrative role. Say, for instance, a corrupt cop that really ruins the fairytale they were setting up for their friends - the evil sheriff gets his due when the hero of the people comes around. Present Talecrafting rules wouldn\u2019t allow it, but it\u2019d be a huge tool for the Seelie the way I envision them.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Second Chance for<br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=709\" data-tempid=\"temp_709_1572548741915_354\" /><br /> A Beautiful Madness</div>","edited":{"userid":425,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","user":"Seraph Kitty","date":". 09-07-2015, 05:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","name":"Seraph Kitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar425_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-08T16:16:03-07:00","id":657128,"title":"","content":"You're welcome! I'm glad you found things helpful, and I'm happy to continue the conversation.<br /> <br /> Hunt 1 + 2<br /> They way you're describing Hunt 1, it sounds more like &quot;wind up in the same place as your target&quot; than &quot;follow a target&quot;, which I think is a distinct difference from Directions 1. That didn't come through in the mechanics, though. If that's the goal, I would re-do it (below) as a 2-dot, and swap it with the existing Hunt 2. (Having Spotting the Vulnerable be hit-or-miss is okay for a 1-dot, I think.)<br /> <br /> Tracing the Fading Footsteps (\u2022\u2022)<br /> Use of this Clause ensures that the changeling will be able to find the target again, once they\u2019ve chosen them to be taken by the Others.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> Cost: 1 Glamour<br /> Dice Pool: Survival + Wyrd versus Wits + Stealth<br /> Action: Instant and Contested (resistance is reflexive)<br /> Duration: One Season<br /> Catch: The target has an active pledge with the changeling.<br /> ROLL RESULTS<br /> Dramatic Failure: The changeling marks another character within eyesight instead of their chosen target.<br /> Failure: The target is unmarked, and the changeling cannot try again for the scene.<br /> Success: The changeling touches the target, mystically marking them. For the next season, the changeling may spend 1 Glamour to encounter the target in the following scene. The changeling does not know exactly where the target will be, but will be guided there on wings of chance. Often, they will follow the target\u2019s most commonly (or most recently) used route to the their location, but sometimes the Wyrd gives more circuitous directions. The changeling will know when they are approaching line-of-sight with the target, allowing them to stop before they are noticed.<br /> This Clause cannot be used to track targets who are not currently on the same plane of existence.<br /> Exceptional Success: As success, and the changeling will get some insight into where the target is at the moment they begin following the target.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div> <br /> Hunt 4<br /> Yaaaaaaay <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> It is similar to Thorns &amp; Briers 4 or Punishing Summer 3, but I'm glad it works with your intention. Although I've realized the Dramatic Failure is ambiguous; it should be &quot;A barrier springs up, but not where the user intended (separating them and their target, or trapping them with their pursuers). The barrier is treated as having two success.&quot;<br /> <br /> Hunt 5<br /> With a pool of Wyrd and Occult, I'd expect three successes regularly (since Occult is a fairly popular/useful skill). Having it be contested (rather than resisted) also means that the user doesn't have to figure out the highest Resolve of those affected; those affected can all roll on their own. I don't know if witnesses should count as &quot;closely affected&quot; or not; I could see that being situational (someone witnessing what looks like a hit would count; someone seeing the user bringing a &quot;drunk&quot; friend home wouldn't). For memory recovery, I'd have it be two attributes, or attribute + skill, rather than just Resolve. (Intelligence + Composure is the standard memory roll.)<br /> <br /> Dominion 4<br /> Successes bounding value is an interesting way of limiting it. For buyable objects, it's easy; I would have trouble coming up with a value of more esoteric things. I don't think that also having a penalty for that would make sense. Also, what happens with fewer successes? If I'm trying to create a chainsaw for my friend (a 4-dot item), what happens if I get less? Do I fail? Do I make something less useful (an axe)? Also also, does the favor get the target into more trouble (outside of owing the user)? Can the clause only be used to solve smaller problems? From your explanation, it sounds like yes to both, but the mechanics and written flavor don't imply that.<br /> <br /> Dominion 5<br /> If it's limited to people who had an excellent impression of the changeling, that's enough of a limit to not need a time delay. Alternatively, having it work as: user betrays target, user uses clause, target gains excellent impression for a week, impression magically decays, target is even more angry would fit a different, but similar, narrative. <br /> <br /> If it's not an obvious magical manipulation (I thought it was), I would add some limit on how often it can be used on a target (since &quot;I betrayed you for a good reason&quot; will work one time, but probably not more than once on the same person). Maybe once a target? Or two times (because the third betrayal is the last straw). <br /> <br /> Fleeting Dreams 2<br /> Okay, that wasn't clear. Attempt at rewording for clarity is below.<br /> <br /> Success: The glamour wraps around the target, and invests a very subtle Mask (or a subtle change to the Mask, in the case of changeling targets) which causes anyone they interact with to feel the designated emotion towards them. This manifests as a +/- 2 modifier to the target\u2019s social interactions. Those affected by the emotion can roll a reflexive Composure + Empathy to identify and resist acting on the emotion (causing the target to lose their bonus/penalty).<br /> <br /> Fleeting Dreams 3<br /> Gotcha. Mechanically, I don't think that will happen as-written. It sounds kinda like the inverse of the Convince the Dreamer action when dream-riding, but instead of biasing a target towards you, you're biasing a target for or against someone/something else. Maybe writing it in that context would be clearer? Proposal below (it does get rid of the Delusion Condition/Derangement, sorry).<br /> <br /> Answers in Dreams (\u2022\u2022\u2022)<br /> By manipulating the dreams of the target, the changeling can create associations between feelings, situations, individuals, and belief. Most oneiromancers can only bias a dreamer when the oneiromancer encounters them in the real world; using this clause, however, lets a Seelie courtier manipulate a target's impression of others.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> Cost: 2 Glamour<br /> Dice Pool: Intelligence + Empathy + Mantle vs Resolve + Wyrd<br /> Action: Instant, contested (resistance is reflexive)<br /> Catch: The target was suffering from exhaustion penalties before falling asleep.<br /> ROLL RESULTS<br /> Dramatic Failure: The changeling creates a negative association between them and the target. This association is permanent, but can be worn down over time. Further, the target becomes immune to the changeling's use of this power.<br /> Failure: The changeling fails to create an emotional association through the target\u2019s dreams. They may not attempt to influence the same target again until the next time they sleep - although other targets can be influenced.<br /> Success: The user creates a positive or negative association between the target and someone or something else. For positive associations, the target will seek out the associated thing; for negative associations, the target will try to avoid it. If the association is with a person, that person gets +/-2 to all Social rolls against the target (based on if the association was positive or negative). This association lasts for days equal to successes. Multiple uses on a target to create the same association with the same thing may permanently affect the dreamer, at the ST's discretion.<br /> Exceptional Success: As success, but the association is permanent. Permanent associations can be worn down over time by outside influence.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div> <br /> Fleeting Dreams 4<br /> This power is almost exactly Fleeting Autumn 4, changing &quot;fears&quot; to &quot;hopes,&quot; limiting to a single target as an instant action (rather than multiple as an extended), letting the user ignore the effects, and extending the duration. Maybe having the user ignore the effects is too much, but it felt like the kind of loophole that the first Seelie would build in, and seems like a fair trade for multiple targets. The duration is longer because I felt like goal/hope manipulation happens less often than getting scared. It could be dropped back to scene/day rather than day/week, but I worry that would make the power much less useful. I would assume that &quot;mundane attempts to change goals/hopes&quot; include the target changing their mind about a goal/hope, but that could be stated more explicitly (and be more obvious as a drawback). <br /> <br /> Fleeting Dreams 5<br /> My proposal is short, yeah. I worry about this being used on players (and dictating their actions), thus why I made it a short-term effect. I could see a longer term one, but needing some way to resist (spending Willpower, probably; I like that better than forcing a roll). So if it lasted for a scene, spend Will to act out of character for an action; if it lasted a day, spend Will for the scene, etc. <br /> <br /> I know very little about Talecrafting, so I can't really critique your version further.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5695-aceofalmonds","name":"AceOfAlmonds","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5695_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"323"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-09T17:06:38-07:00","id":658649,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><b>Hunt 1 / 2</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">I always got the impression that Directions 1 would lead you directly to a target anyway: it gives you a straight line to where they are, and constantly updates for an hour - at which point, you could use it again, if that\u2019s not enough to reach their location. But it doesn\u2019t really help you find out where they\u2019ve been.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">This power is (somewhat) intended to be the inverse. It\u2019s supposed to be more akin to picking a target at a mall, then following them home - only they can start following at any later time. There\u2019s additional insight into the target which can be gleamed from tracing their route, rather than just going straight to them, and it does take you to their location (or where they will be), but at a much slower pace, and with no inkling of where they might be when you start stalking.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">With that goal, though, I think I want to change the exceptional success from \u201cgain insight to where they are before starting\u201d (which I had done to allow players to go immediately there, rather than slowly follow in their footsteps) to instead reading: \u201cthe changeling also notices the places where the target spend significant time\u201d and/or \u201chad intense emotions\u201d.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><b>Dominion 4</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">Hmm, you make a good point; that is a bit tricky. I think I may have to rework this Clause all together\u2026</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">My intent with it was to curse the target while earning their trust and putting them in the character\u2019s debt, by offering a gift which solves a problem, and creates another problem. Kind of like the \u201cTrading Luck For Fate\u201d goblin contract, I guess, but applied to another character and used explicitly to make them owe you.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">I do like the utility of being able to produce an object or information which would solve the target\u2019s Aspiration, and I would like it to have variability / flexibility to it - being able to produce money, weapons, clips of information (name, password). </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">But I wouldn\u2019t want it to be used to arm your allies, or suddenly twist a scene on it\u2019s head by, say, pulling a chainsaw out your pocket; and I wouldn\u2019t want it to handwave character\u2019s long-term aspirations or plot points. </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">There should definitely be a limit to size and complexity and utility, and it should be more difficult to produce rare or exclusive things than common ones\u2026</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">It\u2019s also not meant to be used repetitively; it may get several good uses against a single target, but it\u2019s meant to set the stage for other types of manipulation. Kind of a \u201cfoot in the door\u201d trick to easily get a stranger in the Unseelie\u2019s pocket.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">The main benefit of the contract is meant to be the social one where, whether or not the changeling demands a payment, the target feels indebted by the offer of help. In 2e rules, I might say that use of the Clause automatically opens a Social Door (1-3?) as if the target owed the character and/or as if the use of the Contract fulfilled an Aspiration (I don\u2019t know if there\u2019s a ruling for that; I haven\u2019t read the Doors system very closely).</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">Ideally it would be used to get people a goal they <i>think</i> they want, and the gift itself would end up getting them into more trouble - but not in a way that implicates the changeling who used the Contract. [example: <i>person wants a gun to warn off thugs; gets picked up by the police for carrying without a license</i>.] But sometimes that strains credulity, and I wouldn\u2019t want the gift to <i>need</i><span style=\"color:#000000\">to cause the next problem; circumstances twist regardless to; easiest if it\u2019s in a repetitive manner [example: </span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><i>a string of problems which cost more and more money to fix</i></span><span style=\"color:#000000\">] - but again, I wouldn\u2019t really want to restrict what kinds of problems come up, for those cases where that kind of pattern wouldn\u2019t make sense.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Those are my thoughts for what I\u2019m </span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><i>trying </i></span><span style=\"color:#000000\">to do with this Clause\u2026. It\u2019s a lot harder and more complicated than I had originally thought the power would be.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><b>Dominion 5</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">The works twice per target rule is a pretty good addition. I might want to make that scale with Willpower and/or how petty or significant the betrayal was, just for fairness, but I like it.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><b>Fleeting Dreams 4</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">Ah, I see; I didn\u2019t catch that connection. I was thinking the duration should be longer than you proposed; it\u2019s not as if there would often be a head\u2019s up that someone\u2019s going to try changing their goals, and it\u2019s not the sort of thing that comes up often, nor does resisting aspiration changes (or failing to) have as drastic consequences as say paralyzing fear. Plus it\u2019s more of a proactive protection - it wouldn\u2019t be effective trying to block a change as it\u2019s happening or help the target shake off the alteration after the fact. I\u2019d say 2 Glamour per day is a pretty hefty cost from that perspective.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><b>Fleeting Dreams 5</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">That is a good point, and it certainly can be used in more harmful ways than running headlong toward something you desire (Fleeting Spring 5). I would go for the longer duration anyway, because, as I said, I\u2019d want it for it\u2019s story impact. But a Willpower to resist briefly would be totally fair in that case.</span></span></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Second Chance for<br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=709\" data-tempid=\"temp_709_1572548741915_354\" /><br /> A Beautiful Madness</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","name":"Seraph Kitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar425_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-21T13:00:12-07:00","id":673358,"title":"","content":"It's come down to three Contracts that I'm struggling to nail down. I have some ideas of what to do with them, but I'm not wholly satisfied, and I'm not sure the best way to move forward.<br /> <br /> <b>Following the Fading Footsteps</b><br /> This has been complicated from the start, and I'm not sure how balanced it is as far as utility and dot rating. The primary goal is a pick out a target and be able to find them later; I do also want to have a stalker theme for it (as that's the 'element' of the Contracts set). So I'm favoring option C, but I don't really want it to be a 2 dot power, and it doesn't meet the goal of actually finding them again....<br /> <br /> <i>Option A (1 dot)</i>: Make it Directions 1. It'll simplify everything, and achieve the goal of the Clause well enough. There is the redundancy issue, which I won't really worry about for my chronicle, as I don't expect anyone to be using Contracts of Directions; in other chronicles, there may be a problem, but they are both Court contracts, and this one is for antagonists [in contrast to Shade and Spirit 1, and Spellbound Autumn 1].<br /> <i>Option B (1.5 dot)</i>: Using the Contract guarantees that the user will find the target next scene, guided by the wings of chance. It's a bit stronger than Directions 1, but doesn't give any insight into where, when, or how they meet with the target. It's cost and limitations can be played around with to be a 1 or a 2 dot, depending on how the next Clause is reworked.<br /> <i>Option C (2 dot)</i>: The version I really like (for meeting the stalker theme) is the effect of retracing whatever movement the target did last scene. It might (but is not guaranteed) to catch the changeling up with the target, it gives additional insight into where the target goes frequently - so adds some nice personal knowledge that can be used in other ways, it's fun and unique and fits with Loyalist changelings. But I really don't want it to be a 2 dot, mostly because of the following Clause...<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Spotting the Vulnerable</b><br /> Detecting weakness is well and good, useful and fitting - but there is a <i>long</i> list of weaknesses out there. I could treat it like any Fleeting Seasonal Contract (learning 1 target's weaknesses) and it would work well enough. However, I really wanted the main use of this Clause to help the loyalist / privateer pick the best victim out of a crowd, and that's run me into trouble with it being complicated, and having too many unlikely weaknesses.<br /> <br /> <i>Option A (1 dot)</i>: read 1 target's weaknesses. It fits with similar contracts, it's simple, it lets me do what I like with the former Clause. It doesn't meet the main goal of helping choose a target out of a crowd, but it can be spammed reasonably - and be valuable each time.<br /> <i>Option B (1 dot)</i>: Name a specific Vice, Flaw, or Derangement to locate in a crowd. As Ace said, it can be given a low cost and a high chance of failure (ie, no one in the crowd has that one), and it'd be fitting for a 1 dot. That's frustrating, though, and I don't want players to burn through Glamour without any chance of success.<br /> <i>Option C (2 dot)</i>: As it currently is - the above option, but refunding Glamour if they succeed and the designated weakness isn't found. It still forces players to spam it, fruitlessly going through the list of weaknesses, but it doesn't make it a burdensome cost. The refund, though, definitely puts it in 2 dot territory. It's weak, but serviceable.<br /> <i>Option D (2 dot)</i>: Name a category of weakness or a situation you're looking for an advantage in, and spot those in a crowd it would apply to. It keeps the flavor, meets my original goals, and makes the cost more bearable by making each search broader (thus narrowing the potential list). The problem is that makes it incredibly vague, and entirely in the realm of Storyteller discretion - something that drives me nuts personally, and would be much better suited to another game system like Fate.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Gift of Entrapment</b><br /> This turned out to be a weird gift - simple in concept, but difficult in execution, and a possible Pandora's box for suddenly-ruined plots. I don't know how to reign this in and keep the concept, which I am really fond of.<ul><li>For utility: I've always intended that it could be used for flat cash, getting simple information (like a name or location, possibly a password), producing a handgun or simpler weapon.... Ideally, it solves a short-term, mundane goal or problem that the target has. But I don't want it to solve plotlines, or give the players everything they need, or those kind of issues. It's really meant to be used against NPC's, for mundane stuff, and specifically against strangers in order to get them to think favorably of the changeling. It is a 4 dot, so some utility for the motley is fine with me, it's just needs to be emphasized as a risky move. That's a really vague description though, and very difficult to explain well.</li> <li>The main goal is the social benefit, so that will be more explicitly stated: it will increase the target's impression of the user by 1; exceptional success also removes a door from the next social maneuver against the target. In 1E terms, it'll probably remove any penalties, make the target think of the user as someone who can help them, and grant, say, 9-again to the next Social roll against them with an exceptional success.</li> <li>I'm currently thinking of applying a penalty equal to the merit dots it would cost to obtain (usually resources dots, but maybe also status or contacts, etc).</li> <li>Also putting in explicit limits in size, complexity, and utility - if I can figure out good ways to do that.</li> </ul>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Second Chance for<br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=709\" data-tempid=\"temp_709_1572548741915_354\" /><br /> A Beautiful Madness</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","name":"Seraph Kitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar425_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-27T16:31:08-07:00","id":680505,"title":"","content":"I've reworked my three troublesome Clauses, and think that I have good final versions for how I want these Contracts to look. Critique of these new versions would be greatly appreciated.<br /> <br /> Following the Fading Footsteps (Hunt \u2022)<br /> Use of this Clause allows a changeling mark a target with glamour, allowing them to track the target later by the lingering ripples of emotion.<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Cost: 1 Glamour to mark + 1 Glamour to follow<br /> Dice Pool: Survival + Wyrd versus Composure + Stealth<br /> Action: Instant and Contested (resistance is reflexive)<br /> Duration: 3 days<br /> Catch: The changeling marks an object instead, and gifts it to the target. Doing so means they need not spend Glamour to start tracking, however they follow the object\u2019s trail, not the person\u2019s.<br /> ROLL RESULTS<br /> Dramatic Failure: The changeling marks another nearby person instead of their chosen target. If they gave an object to their intended target, it immediately changes hands through the whim of Fate, unbeknownst to the changeling.<br /> Failure: The changeling is unable to mark the target to be followed, but won\u2019t be aware of this until they attempt to do so.<br /> Success: The changeling choose a target whom they can see and marks them with Glamour. While the Glamour mark persists (3 days), it leaves a trail of emotion which the changeling is able to track, radiating off of the target. The changeling must spend another point of Glamour each time they begin tracking, to attune himself to the particular trail; once they do so, they are allowed to make tracking rolls to follow the target, using their ability to sense emotions rather than by mundane forms of tracking. The changeling can only hone their senses in this way for an hour at a time - requiring additional glamour if they wish to continue.<br /> The Glamour trail is only left behind in the mortal realm; in the Hedge and Arcadia, the ambient Glamour overwhelms any trail left behind; other planes operate on different laws, in which Glamour leaves no lasting mark.<br /> Exceptional Success: Finely attuned to the echoes of Glamour from their target, the changeling will recognize the places that the target spend considerable time or felt strong emotions along their route.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Spotting the Vulnerable (Hunt \u2022\u2022)<br /> The Loyalist on the hunt uses this Clause to search out a particular weakness in the crowd, helping them better select the ideal victim for their needs.<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Cost: 1 Glamour + 2 Glamour (optional)<br /> Dice Pool: Wits + Wyrd versus (highest) Resolve + Subterfuge<br /> Action: Extended and Contested (resistance is reflexive); the amount of time is chosen by the changeling when the Clause is used (see suggested modifiers below)<br /> Catch: The changeling poses a riddle or a leading question to the group; it must be at least related to the trait they\u2019re looking for, but need not be delivered directly.<br /> ROLL RESULTS<br /> Dramatic Failure: The changeling focuses in on a detail which they misinterpret for the weakness in question; the target they find not only isn\u2019t vulnerable to the weakness being sought, but likely has a significant advantage that the changeling isn\u2019t aware of.<br /> Failure: The changeling finds no one which has the weakness they\u2019re seeking, and isn\u2019t certain if they overlooked something or if no such target it present.<br /> Success: The changeling chooses a specific type of weakness which they try to seek out in a crowd. They may be looking for a physical disadvantage, a compulsive behavior, someone who is trusting, or someone who could be bribed or lured away - or similar categories or situations where the weakness would apply. Upon a successful roll in the contest, any individuals to whom the type of weakness may apply stand out to the changeling. Greater successes in the contest reveal increasingly hidden disadvantages: one success may reveal those who are currently suffering from or acting on a weakness; three successes may reveal those for whom the weakness could apply an immediate disadvantage, but which currently isn\u2019t obvious; five or more may be needed to notice those with weaknesses that don\u2019t have visible signs, or which are subtle or abstract. If at any point, the crowd\u2019s resistance roll reduces the changeling\u2019s net successes, they lose track of individuals which had previously been revealed, with no clear way of finding them again.<br /> Once the changeling has noticed individuals to whom the type of weakness may apply, they may choose to select one target and pay the optional cost to learn the details about their particular weakness. By doing so, the changeling ends the Clause, and loses track of the other individuals that had been revealed.<br /> The Storyteller may decide which types of weakness are too broad or too narrow for the purposes of this Contract, and may choose to levy additional penalties based on the type and it\u2019s difficult to notice.<br /> Exceptional Success: If the changeling earns 5 or more net successes in a single roll, they may choose to end the Clause and reveal only the most exploitable individual with that type of weakness.<br /> Suggested Modifiers: time per roll: one action -3, one minute -2, five minutes -1, ten minutes 0, half hour +1, one hour +2. Size of crowd: five or fewer +2, about 20 -0, about 100 -2. Category: physical -0, social -1, mental -2, supernatural -3. </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Gift of Entrapment (Dominion \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022)<br /> The changeling spins together an object their target desires out of scraps and Glamour, earning their trust with a gift, while driving them further into reliance on the changeling\u2019s help.<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Cost: 2 Glamour + 1 Willpower<br /> Dice Pool: Crafts + Wyrd (versus Resolve + Empathy; optional)<br /> Action: Instant; contested at the time of the next misfortune (optional)<br /> Catch: The target expresses a wish or desire out loud before they notice the changeling.<br /> ROLL RESULTS<br /> Dramatic Failure: The changeling produces an object which the target apparently could use to accomplish what they desire. The Glamour holds for just long enough for them to attempt to use it, at which point it is revealed as scraps - which itself may get them into further trouble. The target knows the changeling peddled fool\u2019s gold to them.<br /> Failure: The changeling can create nothing useful. Repeated attempts suffer a cumulative -1 penalty. The target may also stop believing that they can assist, and ignore them.<br /> Success: The changeling produces an object, woven out of scraps of junk and Glamour, which the target can use to accomplish their goal - preferably to get them out of some kind of trouble. The changeling must at least have general knowledge of what type of object would be helpful, however neither the changeling nor the target has control of what the object will be; it will be a simple, commonly available object which can help the target. Creating something unique or supernatural is beyond the scope of this Clause.<br /> If the target accepts this gift, it becomes as good as the real thing, and does indeed help them accomplish whatever goal they were aiming for. The target immediately has an Excellent impression of the changeling, and will for the next week (or the remainder of the Chapter) and the target gains the Leveraged condition toward the changeling [1e: the changeling gains 9-again in Social interactions with the target for the next week]. Even if the target rejects the offer, they are impressed by the changeling\u2019s goodwill: they step up to a Excellent Impression [1e: the changeling gains +3 to Social rolls with the target for a week].<br /> The gift, though useful to the target\u2019s goals, hides a curse which brings them back to the changeling for help. Anyone who benefits from this Clause is cursed to suffer another misfortune soon after, created by the circumstances of Fate. The misfortune won\u2019t implicate the changeling; instead the target will think of the changeling as someone who can help with these circumstances as well. The target may choose to roll Resolve + Empathy to resist asking for the changeling for further help, and must beat the successes earned on the activation of this Contract in order to resist.<br /> This Clause may again be used to aide in the misfortune caused by this curse, however the curse is cumulative; it becomes increasingly more harmful or inconveniencing to the target, and causes cumulative -1 penalty to the use of this Clause; the changeling begins to become the target of misfortune as well. Repeated use of this Contract may delay the curse for a time, but it will eventually catch up with them, and may be deadly to both the target and the changeling by the time it does.<br /> Exceptional Success: The target instead gains a Perfect impression of the changeling [1e, the changeling gains 8-again on Social rolls toward the target]. This benefit lasts for 1 day per -1 penalty on the roll.<br /> Suggested Modifiers: -1 per Merit dot that would be required to obtain the item by mundane means (most often, this will be Resources).</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Second Chance for<br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=709\" data-tempid=\"temp_709_1572548741915_354\" /><br /> A Beautiful Madness</div>","edited":{"userid":425,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","user":"Seraph Kitty","date":". 09-28-2015, 09:42 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","name":"Seraph Kitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar425_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}